WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.679
The capability that takes an LLM from generating text to actually doing things in the world

2
00:00:05.679 --> 00:00:11.279
is tool use. For this video, we'll walk through the full life cycle of a tool call,

3
00:00:11.279 --> 00:00:16.879
look at how description quality affects selection reliability, and draw the line between a

4
00:00:16.879 --> 00:00:22.000
developer-scripted tool sequence and a model-driven agentic one. Let's get into it.

5
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.440
Every tool a model ever touches passes through the same short exchange,

6
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:33.200
and each part of it has a name worth knowing. A tool definition is a schema you pass to the

7
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:40.000
model at inference time. It has a name, a natural language description, and a parameter specification.

8
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.520
The model reads this to figure out what the tool does and whether it applies to the current task.

9
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:52.080
A tool call is the structured output the model emits when it decides to use a tool. It's not

10
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:58.560
plain text. It's a formatted object containing the tool name and a JSON argument object. An

11
00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:05.120
observation is what comes back after the runtime executes the call. It gets injected into the next

12
00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:11.440
context window, and the model uses it to plan its next step. That exchange is the spine of every

13
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:18.080
agentic interaction. Let's make this concrete at the API level because the round trip is specific.

14
00:01:18.639 --> 00:01:24.239
When Claude decides it needs a tool, the response comes back with a stop reason of tool use,

15
00:01:24.239 --> 00:01:30.559
and the content includes one or more tool use blocks. Each block names the tool and carries

16
00:01:30.559 --> 00:01:36.959
the arguments in JSON. That's Claude's half of the exchange. Your half is to run the tool

17
00:01:36.959 --> 00:01:43.440
and send back a tool result block that references the same call. One distinction lives here. For

18
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:50.400
client tools, the ones you define, your application executes the call. For server tools,

19
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:56.080
like web search, Anthropic runs the tool for you and returns the result directly.

20
00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:01.680
Either way, that tool result becomes the observation Claude reads on its next turn.

21
00:02:02.319 --> 00:02:08.080
This exchange is the atomic unit of the whole loop, and it repeats many times in a single task.

22
00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.559
Here's the distinction that separates a workflow from an agent, stated as precisely as possible.

23
00:02:14.559 --> 00:02:19.759
In a pre-scripted workflow, the developer decides at design time which tools run,

24
00:02:19.759 --> 00:02:25.039
and in what order. The model might fill in the parameters, but it never chooses the tool.

25
00:02:25.039 --> 00:02:31.679
The sequence is fixed. In a model-driven agentic system, the model selects tools at inference time,

26
00:02:31.679 --> 00:02:36.559
based on what it's trying to accomplish right now. The sequence isn't known in advance.

27
00:02:37.039 --> 00:02:43.440
It varies across runs based on context and intermediate results. That runtime selection

28
00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.559
is the defining characteristic of agentic behavior. A system where the developer controls

29
00:02:48.559 --> 00:02:54.160
the tool order is a workflow, full stop, even if it involves an LLM at every step.

30
00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:01.440
Tool description quality is one of the most underappreciated factors in agentic reliability.

31
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.759
When Claude needs to decide whether to use a tool, it reads the description,

32
00:03:05.759 --> 00:03:12.080
not the implementation. If the description is vague, like processes data, Claude has no reliable

33
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:18.960
basis for selection. If the description is precise, like queries the inventory database by product skew

34
00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:24.800
and returns current stock count, Claude can accurately judge when the tool applies. The

35
00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.679
practical rule is to write descriptions a non-engineer could act on. Describe what the

36
00:03:29.679 --> 00:03:36.479
tool does, what inputs it expects, and what it returns. Don't describe how it works internally.

37
00:03:37.039 --> 00:03:42.080
Poor descriptions are one of the most common root causes of agents, calling the wrong tool

38
00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:47.440
or skipping a tool they should have used. It helps to think about tools in functional

39
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:53.279
categories. Data access tools let the model read from the world, fetching a web page,

40
00:03:53.279 --> 00:03:59.600
querying a database, reading a file, or pulling from a knowledge base. Computation tools let the

41
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:06.320
model do things that pure text generation can't, running code in a sandbox, parsing structured data,

42
00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:13.520
or executing a calculation. External API tools let the model write to the world, sending a message,

43
00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:19.600
creating a calendar event, updating a record, or triggering a downstream workflow. These categories

44
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.959
have different latency profiles, different failure modes, and very different security surfaces.

45
00:04:25.519 --> 00:04:30.320
Knowing which category a tool falls into is the first step in assessing its risk.

46
00:04:31.519 --> 00:04:37.519
The observation phase is where the agentic loop earns its name. A plain pipeline executes steps

47
00:04:37.519 --> 00:04:44.160
in sequence and stops. An agent looks at what came back and decides what to do next. A successful

48
00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:49.679
tool result gets injected into context as a message, and the model moves forward. That

49
00:04:49.679 --> 00:04:55.679
injection step has a name, tool result injection, and it's how every observation reaches the model.

50
00:04:55.679 --> 00:05:01.679
An error triggers replanning. The model might retry with different parameters, try an alternative tool,

51
00:05:01.679 --> 00:05:08.079
or escalate. A partial result might prompt a follow-up call before proceeding. Call, execute,

52
00:05:08.079 --> 00:05:14.239
observe, respond, repeat. That's the tool call loop, and it runs until the agent hits its goal

53
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:20.480
or a stop condition you defined. Let's make this concrete with examples across tool types.

54
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:26.640
Web search. The model calls a search tool, the runtime queries a search API, and ranked results

55
00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:33.760
come back as an observation. File, read, and write. The model reads a document, generates edits, and

56
00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:40.079
writes the updated content back to disk. Code execution. The model writes a Python snippet,

57
00:05:40.079 --> 00:05:46.239
a sandboxed interpreter runs it, and standard output returns as an observation. When these tools

58
00:05:46.239 --> 00:05:51.920
are independent, the model can emit several calls in one response, and the runtime executes them

59
00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:58.799
concurrently, which cuts latency. Each type carries a distinct failure surface. Search returns stale

60
00:05:58.799 --> 00:06:05.519
results. File writes overwrite the wrong target, and a permissive sandbox lets code cause side effects.

61
00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:11.839
When tool use breaks, you rarely see an error. You see the model reaching for the wrong tool,

62
00:06:11.839 --> 00:06:16.880
or skipping one that it should have called. The usual cause is a description written for

63
00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.160
engineers instead of a description written for a model. That's why a field that looks

64
00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:27.839
like documentation is actually the control surface for tool reliability. That wraps up

65
00:06:27.839 --> 00:06:30.239
this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

